>> IAM terms 

1. Principle -- principle is a working entites at the resource. means karta in hindi . for eg : user , application 
2. Request -- Request send via  principle.
3. Authenication -- Valid user. username and password. 
4. Authorization -- level of clearance . 
5. Action/operation -- -- operation requested by principle. 
6. Resources -- action /operation performed at resources. 


1. Principle 
>> A principle is user or application that can make request for action or operation on AWS resource. 
>> Your Adminstrtive user is your first principle. 
>> You can allow user and services to assume  a role. 
>> IAM user, roles, Federated user and application are all AWS principle. 
>> You can  support federated user/user from another organisation or progmmatic access/cli to allow user to access  your AWS account. 


2. Request 
>> 	When a principle tries to use AWS managment console , AWS API or AWS CLI  then principle sends a request to AWS, request contains following 
      a. actions -- that principle wants to perform.
      b. resource -- upon which action are performed.
      c. principle --
      d. enviroment data -- your IP address and your clearance level
      e. resource data 
	  
 >> Request context : Before AWS evalute or authorized a request , it will gather request information. 
      * Principle -- wheather principle is authorized or not and it's clearance level. 
	  * Enviromental data -- IP address , user agent , SSL enabled and time of day. 
	  * Resources data -- data releated to resourece that is being requested. 
	  
3. Authenication 
>> A principle send request must be authenticated  to send request to AWS. 
>> Some AWS services like AWS S3 allows request  from anonyomous  user, they are exception to the role. 
>> To authenticate from console as root user, we must sign in with user name and password. 
>> To access via CLI , then need to have access and secret key. 
>> sometime you must provide additional security information like MFA( Multifactor authentication)   

4. Authorization  : 
>> To authorize a request , IAM uses value from request context to check for matching polices wheather to allow or deny a request. 
>> IAM polices are stored in IAM as JSON documents and specify the permission that are allowed or denied. 
>> user based policies specify permission  for principle. 

Note : By default , only AWS root user have access to all the resources in the account. 
       IAM user ,by default access to  all resource are denied. 

>> Resource based polices -- Implying polices at resource -- for eg applying only read permission at S3 bucket, then principle can only need the content in bucket.
>> IAM check each policy that matches the context of your request. 
>> If a single polices includes a denied action, then IAM denies entire request.This is called explict deny. 
 
*** The evaluation  logic follows  these rules 
>> By default , all request are denied . 
>> An explict allow override the default. 
>> An explict deny override any allow. 
>> An explict deny is more powerful than explict allow. 

5. Action/operation 
>> It is defined by services are the things that can you can do to a resources ie viewing,editing, creating or deleting. 
>> IAM supports approax 40 actions for user. 
>> Any action which are not explicit allow are denied by default. 

6. Resources
>> It is entity within services are resource,for eg , S3 , EC2 instance. 
>> AWS services defined a set of action that can be performed at resource. 
>> If unreleated request to perform a action is requested then AWS will deny that request. for eg : request to install OS at S3. 
>> When you provide permission using identity based policy  in IAM,then you provide access to resource within the same account. 


