** Permission and Policies 
>> IAM helps to define what a user is allowed to do in your account , often refer to as authorization . 
>> Permission are often granted via policies that are created and attached to user, groups and roles. 

Note : Policies applied to user, groups or roles are identity based policies or user based policies . 
     : In identity based policies or user based policies , principle is not in the picture. 
	 
** Policies and users 

>> By default , IAM user can't access anything in your account. 
>> We grant access by defining or creating polices and then attaching those policies to user, group or roles. 
>> Any action of resources which are not explictly allowed are denied by default. 



** IAM multiple policies 
>> User or group can have multiple policies that grant different permission. 
>> In case of multiple policies attached to user then permission are calculated by the combination of those polices. 

** Federated user and roles
>> Federated user doesn't have permanent identity as of IAM user. 
>> To grant permission to federated user we create role and attached them with federated user. 
>> When federated user send request to access resources , then access are granted as per role attached to the user. 

** Resource based polices 
>> In some cases like S3  , you attach a policy to a resource , this is called Resource based polices 
>> Resource based polices are different from user or identity based policies.
    --> In Resource based polices , we specify what actions are permitted and what resources are affected. 
	--> We explictly list , who is allowed access to resource ( Principle )
>> Resource based polices specify principle. 


** AWS recommandations 
>> AWS recommands not to share root user credentials with anyone. 
>> Not to use user root account for day to day work. 
>> To avoid using root user on daily basics, create an IAM user who have all access . 
   Note : IAM user with all access is not same as root user.  
   
